 "Cells" -> {<|"Data" -> ".md\n# FDTD Method\nIn this notebook, we attempt to \
solve Maxwell's equations for a 1D case with two interfaces. This work is \
based on __John B. Schneider's__ *\"Understanding the Finite-Difference \
Time-Domain Method,\"* 2023.\n\nDue to the intensive numerical computations, \
we will use a compiled function constructed from simple C-like building \
blocks.", "Display" -> "codemirror", 
    "Hash" -> "e691f522-9d13-49ce-afd5-708da99ddd77", "Invisible" -> False, 
    "MetaOnly" -> False, "Props" -> <|"Hidden" -> True|>, 
    "PublicFields" -> {"Properties"}, "State" -> "Idle", "Type" -> "Input", 
    "UID" -> Null, "Notebook" -> "50fcefaa-4d88-4ed4-9d3d-87febcecc3af"|>, 
   <|"Data" -> "# FDTD Method\nIn this notebook, we attempt to solve \
Maxwell's equations for a 1D case with two interfaces. This work is based on \
__John B. Schneider's__ *\"Understanding the Finite-Difference Time-Domain \
Method,\"* 2023.\n\nDue to the intensive numerical computations, we will use \
a compiled function constructed from simple C-like building blocks.", 
    "Display" -> "markdown", "Hash" -> 
     "2a5ce5ee-3742-4430-944f-7bab55108406", "Invisible" -> False, 
    "MetaOnly" -> False, "Props" -> <||>, "PublicFields" -> {"Properties"}, 
    "State" -> "Idle", "Type" -> "Output", "UID" -> Null, 
    "Notebook" -> "50fcefaa-4d88-4ed4-9d3d-87febcecc3af"|>, 
   <|"Data" -> ".md\n## Dielectric plate\n\nWe shall start with the simplest \
case, where there are no losses, and our material parameters $\\epsilon$ and \
$\\mu$ are frequency independent (no Drude, no Lorentz response). In this \
scenario, we do not need to write additional equations for polarization and \
currents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{align*}\n\\mu \\frac{\\partial H_{y}}{\\partial t} \
&= \\frac{\\partial E_{z}}{\\partial x} \\\\\\\\\n\\epsilon \\frac{\\partial \
E_{z}}{\\partial t} &= \\frac{\\partial H_{y}}{\\partial x} \
\n\\end{align*}\n$$\n\nThe first equation provides the temporal derivative of \
the magnetic field in terms of the spatial derivative of the electric field. \
Conversely, the second equation provides the temporal derivative of the \
electric field in terms of the spatial derivative of the magnetic field. \
\n\nWe then discretize them:\n\n$$\n\\begin{align*}\nH_{y}[m] = H_{y}[m] + \
\\frac{S}{\\epsilon_r Z} \\Big( E_{z}[m+1] - E_{z}[m] \\Big) \
\\\\\\\\\nE_{z}[m] = E_{z}[m] + \\frac{S Z}{\\mu_r} \\Big( H_{y}[m] - \
H_{y}[m-1] \\Big)\n\\end{align*}\n$$\n\nwhere $S=1.0$ is a time-space number, \
and $Z=\\sqrt{\\mu_0/\\epsilon_0}$ is the vacuum impedance.", 
    "Display" -> "codemirror", "Hash" -> 
     "866009d7-0840-40f0-b54b-bbce4b4079b0", "Invisible" -> False, 
    "MetaOnly" -> False, "Props" -> <|"Hidden" -> True|>, 
    "PublicFields" -> {"Properties"}, "State" -> "Idle", "Type" -> "Input", 
    "UID" -> Null, "Notebook" -> "50fcefaa-4d88-4ed4-9d3d-87febcecc3af"|>, 
   <|"Data" -> "## Dielectric plate\n\nWe shall start with the simplest case, \
where there are no losses, and our material parameters $\\epsilon$ and $\\mu$ \
are frequency independent (no Drude, no Lorentz response). In this scenario, \
we do not need to write additional equations for polarization and \
currents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{align*}\n\\mu \\frac{\\partial H_{y}}{\\partial t} \
&= \\frac{\\partial E_{z}}{\\partial x} \\\\\\\\\n\\epsilon \\frac{\\partial \
E_{z}}{\\partial t} &= \\frac{\\partial H_{y}}{\\partial x} \
\n\\end{align*}\n$$\n\nThe first equation provides the temporal derivative of \
the magnetic field in terms of the spatial derivative of the electric field. \
Conversely, the second equation provides the temporal derivative of the \
electric field in terms of the spatial derivative of the magnetic field. \
\n\nWe then discretize them:\n\n$$\n\\begin{align*}\nH_{y}[m] = H_{y}[m] + \
\\frac{S}{\\epsilon_r Z} \\Big( E_{z}[m+1] - E_{z}[m] \\Big) \
\\\\\\\\\nE_{z}[m] = E_{z}[m] + \\frac{S Z}{\\mu_r} \\Big( H_{y}[m] - \
H_{y}[m-1] \\Big)\n\\end{align*}\n$$\n\nwhere $S=1.0$ is a time-space number, \
and $Z=\\sqrt{\\mu_0/\\epsilon_0}$ is the vacuum impedance.", 
