   <|"Data" -> "(*VB[*)(FrontEndRef[\"cd1c6045-ba1c-457a-98df-0f5825a8c0d1\"]\
)(*,*)(*\"1:eJxTTMoPSmNkYGAoZgESHvk5KRCeEJBwK8rPK3HNS3GtSE0uLUlMykkNVgEKJ6cYJ\
psZmJjqJiUaJuuamJon6lpapKTpGqSZWhiZJlokG6QYAgCJnhXL\"*)(*]VB*)", 
    "Display" -> "codemirror", "Hash" -> 
     "847fde9f-e2a3-4299-90e4-bb358cc9b8d8", "Invisible" -> False, 
    "MetaOnly" -> False, "Props" -> <||>, "PublicFields" -> {"Properties"}, 
    "State" -> "Idle", "Type" -> "Output", "UID" -> Null, 
    "Notebook" -> "50fcefaa-4d88-4ed4-9d3d-87febcecc3af"|>, 
   <|"Data" -> ".md\n## Dielectric with Frequency-Dependent \
Permittivity\n\nLet's explore further by modeling light propagation through a \
medium with frequency-dependent dielectric/magnetic response, such as a \
glass-like material with absorbing dye center impurities. Since we are \
working in the time domain, we cannot simply define \
$\\varepsilon_r(\\omega)$. Instead, we decompose the relative permittivity \
into two coupled parts:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{D} = \\epsilon_0 \\epsilon_r \
\\mathbf{E} = \\epsilon_0 (\\mathbf{E} + \\mathbf{P})\n$$\n\nHere, we write a \
separate equation and discretize it for $\\mathbf{P}$. A good approximation \
for a dielectric is a Lorentz oscillator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial^2 \
\\mathbf{P}}{\\partial t^2} + \\gamma \\frac{\\partial \\mathbf{P}}{\\partial \
t} + \\omega_0^2 \\mathbf{P} = \\Omega \\mathbf{E}\n$$\n\nIn this equation, \
$\\Omega$ is a coupling constant, $\\gamma$ represents losses (proportional \
to optical conductivity), and $\\omega_0$ is the eigenfrequency. By \
introducing a polarization current $\\mathbf{J}$, we can reduce the \
second-order derivative equation to a first-order one, making it suitable for \
the FDTD method.", "Display" -> "codemirror", 
    "Hash" -> "afa975a0-d367-4592-92da-793473699f1a", "Invisible" -> False, 
    "MetaOnly" -> False, "Props" -> <|"Hidden" -> True|>, 
    "PublicFields" -> {"Properties"}, "State" -> "Idle", "Type" -> "Input", 
    "UID" -> Null, "Notebook" -> "50fcefaa-4d88-4ed4-9d3d-87febcecc3af"|>, 
   <|"Data" -> "## Dielectric with Frequency-Dependent Permittivity\n\nLet's \
explore further by modeling light propagation through a medium with \
frequency-dependent dielectric/magnetic response, such as a glass-like \
material with absorbing dye center impurities. Since we are working in the \
time domain, we cannot simply define $\\varepsilon_r(\\omega)$. Instead, we \
decompose the relative permittivity into two coupled \
parts:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{D} = \\epsilon_0 \\epsilon_r \\mathbf{E} = \\epsilon_0 \
(\\mathbf{E} + \\mathbf{P})\n$$\n\nHere, we write a separate equation and \
discretize it for $\\mathbf{P}$. A good approximation for a dielectric is a \
Lorentz oscillator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial^2 \\mathbf{P}}{\\partial t^2} + \
\\gamma \\frac{\\partial \\mathbf{P}}{\\partial t} + \\omega_0^2 \\mathbf{P} \
= \\Omega \\mathbf{E}\n$$\n\nIn this equation, $\\Omega$ is a coupling \
constant, $\\gamma$ represents losses (proportional to optical conductivity), \
and $\\omega_0$ is the eigenfrequency. By introducing a polarization current \
$\\mathbf{J}$, we can reduce the second-order derivative equation to a \
first-order one, making it suitable for the FDTD method.", 
    "Display" -> "markdown", "Hash" -> 
     "60350f0a-1a31-4744-8e7c-8cfefd48165d", "Invisible" -> False, 
    "MetaOnly" -> False, "Props" -> <||>, "PublicFields" -> {"Properties"}, 
    "State" -> "Idle", "Type" -> "Output", "UID" -> Null, 
    "Notebook" -> "50fcefaa-4d88-4ed4-9d3d-87febcecc3af"|>, 
   <|"Data" -> ".md\nHere is a single module for modelling such scenario:", 
    "Display" -> "codemirror", "Hash" -> 
     "c1aa07c6-d61f-43d3-afbb-4932a88b7dcd", "Invisible" -> False, 
    "MetaOnly" -> False, "Props" -> <|"Hidden" -> True|>, 
    "PublicFields" -> {"Properties"}, "State" -> "Idle", "Type" -> "Input", 
